! mcp_bootstrap_p137.strap -- the CONSOLE-MESSAGE buffers, the four
! operator messages, and G EDIT, the backspace editor.  printed page
! 137.  docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.144, header READ and not
! assumed: "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID EE000111
! DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  137".
!
! FIFTY-SIX PRINTED LINES.  The card-serial column runs EE000111
! (line 1) to EE000166 (line 56) -- fifty-six serials for fifty-six
! lines, one per line, read at 400 dpi off the right-hand column.
! Nothing fell off the top or the bottom of the render.  It joins
! printed p.136, whose last serial is EE000110.
!
! WHAT EMITS AND WHAT DOES NOT, counted line by line:
!   32 rows of machine code             lines 1-3, 5-18, 38-46,
!                                       48-51, 54, 56
!    4 DR reservations                  lines 20-23
!    4 entry-mode DD messages           lines 24, 25, 26, 28
!    2 CNOPs                            line 19 (emits a NOP, and
!                                       prints a LOCATION and bits)
!                                       and line 27 (emits nothing
!                                       at all and prints neither)
!   14 comment cards                    lines 4, 29-37, 47, 52, 53,
!                                       55
! 32 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 14 = 56.  FORTY-ONE ROWS CARRY A LOCATION (the 32
! plus the 4 plus the 4 plus line 19).  THIRTY-THREE CARRY MACHINE
! CODE (the 32 plus line 19), which is the !CHECK count below -- the
! four DR rows DO print in the BINARY OUTPUT column, but what they
! print is the reservation's size, not code, so they are not among
! the 33.  See COVERAGE at the bottom.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN IS READ, AND BOTH JOINS ARE WITNESSED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS NO SLC. <<<  216731.00 is line 1's own printed
! LOCATION and it is ALSO printed in the column-heading row, whose
! right-hand field reads "LOCATION      216731" -- RENDERED AND READ
! at 600 dpi (x=7600, y=400), not inherited from p.136's header,
! which carries the same kind of sentence about its own page.  So
! the origin below is a reading of the page twice over -- but a
! hard SLC asserts its value rather than
! checking it (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "A HARD SLC CANNOT
! DISAGREE WITH THE ARRIVING COUNTER"), so THE BACKWARD JOIN IS NOT
! CHECKED BY THIS DECK.
!
! IT IS, HOWEVER, CLOSED BY HAND AND EXACTLY.  decks/mcp_bootstrap_
! p136.strap's line 56 is a full-word connective at 216730.00, so
! that page's counter leaves at 216731.00 -- which is where this
! page's line 1 is printed.  The two pages meet with no gap and no
! overlap, and p.136's deck predicted this join in advance ("(a) The
! survey OCR of printed p.137 reads its line 2 at 216732.00").
!
! THE FORWARD JOIN, AS A PREDICTION printed p.138 CAN FALSIFY.
! Line 56 is a half word at 217056.00, so this page's counter leaves
! at 217056.40.  >>> printed p.138's FIRST row should print
! 217056.40. <<<  Between there and 217063.00 lie four and a half
! words, because the volume OCR carries two p.138 cards whose
! addresses are known independently of this page: "217063.00
! 217063.10 00  G KONEC  B,$" and "217063.40  217012.13 04  G VRAT
! KVI,$5, G MISTO".  So p.138 opens with nine half words of G EDIT's
! tail and then defines G KONEC and G VRAT, the two symbols this
! page's lines 44, 49 and 54 branch to.  NOT CLOSED HERE -- a hard
! SLC cannot check a join -- but written down so that landing p.138
! either confirms it in one line or contradicts it loudly.
!
! THE ADDRESS CHAIN CLOSES END TO END ACROSS ALL FORTY-ONE EMITTING
! ROWS.  Every row's printed address plus its own length is the next
! row's printed address, from 216731.00 to 217056.00, including the
! four DR reservations (13 + 13 + 13 + 10 words) and the four
! entry-mode messages (4 + 4 + 6 + 3 words).  That is the single
! strongest check on this page and it is what pins the message
! texts character for character -- see below.
!
! TAIL SCOPE.  printed p.99 line 11's "SLC,(8)215000." moved the
! origin here and pp.99+ are OUTSIDE the TAIL,(2)G block that printed
! p.2 opened and printed p.98 line 37 closed with "UNTAIL,(2)".
! Names defined here may legitimately repeat names from pp.2-98 at
! other addresses; none of this page's fifteen does.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE CONTAINS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Lines 1-18   the tails of three short routines, all of which
!              printed p.136 branches into or out of.
!              Line 1 unselects the MCP unit (S SEL-1.0, one word
!              below the S SEL that p.136 lines 36-37 select) and
!              line 2 jumps back to P EX1 on p.136.
!              Line 3 is P MP LEV, the MCP-level interrupt entry
!              that p.136 line 53 branches to: it loads S IC BU and
!              falls into P COM.
!              Lines 6-8 are G ERROR (p.136 line 47's target): point
!              $1 at the "PP HAS NO CONSOLE IOD" message, set $2 to
!              its 6-word length, and branch to P ERR EX on p.135.
!              Lines 9-11 are G ODBOC, the same three steps for the
!              3-word "MESSAGE HAS BEEN ERASED".
!              Lines 12-18 are P DBRET, the DEBUGGER-side return:
!              index P CDEF2 (the debugger console IOD) off S BAMCP,
!              fetch the activated file-area address into S FI AAC,
!              and -- if S L says so -- rejoin P MAIN on p.136,
!              otherwise load S IC BU and branch to P MAIN1.
! Lines 20-23  FOUR BUFFER RESERVATIONS, which is where p.136's four
!              SYNs were pointing all along: P TEM BF (13 words),
!              P PP BF (13), P MCP BF (13) and G MISTO (10).
! Lines 24-28  THE FOUR OPERATOR MESSAGES, entry-mode DD cards in
!              the 89-character set with "*" as the end-of-statement
!              character.
! Lines 38-56  G EDIT, THE BACKSPACE EDITOR.  Its nine comment cards
!              (29-37) state the contract: "ITS FCN IS TO EDIT OUT
!              FROM A STRING OF 8 BIT IQS CHARACTERS ALL BACK SPACE
!              CHARACTERS.  FOR EACH BACKSPACE CHARACTER BOTH IT AND
!              THE PREVIOUS NON BACKSPACE CHARACTER ARE REMOVED FROM
!              THE STRING.  THE UNEDITED STRING OF CHARACTERS MUST BE
!              LESS THAN 81 CHARACTERS LONG.  AT THE CONCLUSION OF
!              THE EDITING, THE CF OF $4 CONTAINS THE NUMBER OF
!              CHARACTERS IN THE EDITED MESSAGE."
!              G OKOLO (line 42) is the loop: load a character with
!              progressive indexing, compare it against backspace
!              (2)1111 1100 -- go to G VRAT if so -- otherwise store
!              it, count it, and compare against the end code
!              (2)1111 1110.  G KONTR (line 51) is the 80-character
!              overrun test.  Line 56 puts the edited count into $R.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE FOUR MESSAGE CARDS, AND WHY THEIR BLANKS ARE NOT GUESSED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> IN ENTRY MODE THE BLANK AFTER THE COMMA IS DATA, AND SO IS
! EVERY BLANK BEFORE THE TERMINATOR. <<<  The cards print as
!
!   (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED EPGK ERROR*
!   (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED UK ERROR  *
!   (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED PP HAS NO CONSOLE IOD     *
!   (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE HAS BEEN ERASED*
!
! and the BINARY OUTPUT column is EMPTY for all four -- the listing
! prints no bits for a converted DD this long -- so no !CHECK can
! read them.  What checks them instead is the LOCATION column, and
! it checks them exactly:
!
!   P EPGK  217024.00   32 chars = 4 words -> P UK at 217030.00
!   P UK    217030.00   32 chars = 4 words -> P UNNOT at 217034.00
!   P UNNOT 217034.00   48 chars = 6 words -> P MSGER at 217042.00
!   P MSGER 217042.00   24 chars = 3 words -> G EDIT  at 217045.00
!
! At 8 bits per character (the byte size is omitted, which C28-6129
! printed p.16 reads modulo 8 as eight) the character count IS the
! address arithmetic, so a single wrong blank anywhere in any of the
! four moves every later symbol on the page.  MEASURED: assembled on
! its own, the four-card block puts all four symbols on their printed
! addresses.  The counts are
!   " MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED EPGK ERROR"              32, no padding
!   " MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED UK ERROR" + 2 blanks     32
!   " MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED PP HAS NO CONSOLE IOD"
!                                   + 5 blanks      48
!   " MESSAGE HAS BEEN ERASED"                      24, no padding
! and the two padded ones were also read off the page at 600 dpi as
! a two-character and a five-character gap before the "*".
!
! >>> WHAT THE LOCATION COLUMN PINS IS THE LENGTH, AND ONLY THE
! LENGTH. <<<  Seventeen words of English text sit inside these four
! cards and NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY CHECKS A SINGLE CHARACTER OF
! THEM.  "ACCEPTED" against "ACCEPTEC", "CONSOLE" against "CCNSCLE",
! "NOT" against "NCT" -- every one of this drum's round-glyph
! failures produces a string of the SAME LENGTH, so it lands every
! later symbol on its printed address and passes.  The only thing
! holding the characters is that the column is ENGLISH and a
! misprinted letter inside a known word is visible as a misspelling
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "THE COMMENT COLUMN IS ENGLISH").
! They were read at 600 dpi and every word is an ordinary English
! word; that is the whole of the evidence, and it is weaker than
! what backs any !CHECK row above.
!
! THE ENTRY MODE ITSELF IS "(IQS*)", the 89-character set with "*"
! chosen as the end-of-statement character (C28-6129 printed p.16:
! the last character of the field is the terminator and is the
! programmer's choice).  strap_asm implements IQS; it does NOT
! implement MCP's other entry mode "(CC*)", so this page is the
! first to exercise IQS entry mode on a real page.  No substitution
! was needed: the printed form assembles as printed.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE FIFTEEN NAMES THIS PAGE DEFINES, AND WHAT SETTLES EACH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SEVEN ARE CONFIRMED OFF-PAGE BY A LANDED DECK, which is the
! strongest witness available for a SPELLING (no !CHECK tests one).
! decks/mcp_bootstrap_p136.strap SYNs each of these at exactly the
! address this page prints for it:
!   P MP LEV 216732.40   p.136 line 53's BB target
!   G ERROR  216733.40   p.136 line 47's BB target
!   P TEM BF 216743.00   p.136 lines 34 and 52
!   P PP BF  216760.00   p.136 line 52
!   P MCP BF 216775.00   p.136 lines 31 and 34
!   G MISTO  217012.00   p.136 lines 9 and 31
!   G EDIT   217045.00   p.136 line 2
! TWO MORE ARE CONFIRMED OFF-PAGE BY printed p.135's landed deck:
!   G ODBOC  216735.00   p.135 line 48, "BZAE,G ODBOC"
!   P EPGK   217024.00   p.135 line 27, "LVI,$1, P EPGK"
! and p.135's deck also names P DBRET (216736.40) from its own line
! 54 and quotes this page's line 12 as that symbol's defining card.
!
! >>> G ODBOC: THE SPELLING IS NOT MINE AND IS NOT A GUESS. <<<  The
! name is round-D-B-round-round and this drum opens round glyphs, so
! shape alone cannot settle it, AND THIS PAGE'S OWN INK DID NOT
! SETTLE IT HERE: at 1800 dpi line 9's glyphs 1 and 4 both read as
! open rounds and glyph 5 as an open round, which under the dropout
! asymmetry is compatible with O-D-B-O-C and with C-D-B-C-C alike.
! What settles it is measurement done elsewhere.
! decks/mcp_bootstrap_p135.strap
! measured mean ink in the right-hand band of each glyph at 1600 dpi
! on BOTH printings -- p.135 line 48 and THIS page's line 9 -- and
! got O=.07 D=.31 B=.63 O=.08 C=.00 here against O=.12 .. O=.55
! C=.00 there; position 5 measures exactly .00 on both pages, which
! no O does.  Its second, independent reading is the wordlist: this
! programmer's G-labels are Czech -- G KONEC (konec, end), G ZPET
! (zpet, back), G VRAT (vrat, return), G MISTO (misto, place),
! G OKOLO (okolo, around), G KONTR (kontr-) -- and "odboc" is
! "branch off", which is what line 9 does.
!
! >>> G OKOLO (line 42) IS SETTLED BY ITS OWN REFERENCE ON THIS
! PAGE. <<<  Line 42's NAME column reads "G CKOLC"-ish at 1800 dpi;
! line 50's "CB,$5,G OKOLO" in the STATEMENT column, two inches
! away and two words of ink later, prints glyphs 1 and 3 as plainly
! CLOSED bowls and glyph 5 with a nick in the upper right.  Dropout
! opens a closed glyph and never closes an open one, so the closed
! ones are O.  The wordlist agrees, and the volume OCR of p.135's
! agent had flagged this very symbol as "probably OKOLO, around" and
! declined to call it without rendering it; it is rendered here.
!
! >>> P UK (217030.00) IS THE ONE NAME WITH NO WITNESS ANYWHERE.
! <<<  Nothing on this page references it and the full-volume OCR
! has no hit at 217030 and no hit on the name.  Its two glyphs are
! clean at 1200 dpi (U with both stems, K unambiguous) and the
! message beside it reads "MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED UK ERROR", which is
! the same two letters in English -- and UK is Reference Manual
! indicator 10 (strap/opcodes.py INDICATOR_BITS), the Unending
! Operation / Unconditional-K indicator, exactly as EPGK on the line
! above is indicator 9.  So the PAIR is named after two adjacent
! machine indicators, which is as close to a second reading as this
! symbol has.  Its LOCATION is checked (by P UNNOT's); its SPELLING
! rests on ink plus that argument.
!
! P UNNOT (217034.00) and P MSGER (217042.00) are each referenced a
! second time ON THIS PAGE, by lines 6 and 9, which print them as
! "P UN NOT" and "P MSG ER"; the comments beside the messages
! ("PP HAS NO CONSOLE IOD", "MESSAGE HAS BEEN ERASED") are the
! English oracle for the round glyphs in NOT and MSG.
! G KONTR (217055.00) has an off-page reference in the volume OCR
! at printed p.138, "217070.40  21?055.16 C0  B,G KONTR".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS.  WHAT IS READ AND WHAT IS CONVENTION
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> AN ADDRESS BACK-DERIVED FROM A PRINTED BRANCH OR LVI IS PINNED
! ONLY TO THE HALF WORD. <<<  Bit offsets 0-31 all encode
! identically; only bit 18, the half-word select, shows, and it is
! the TOP bit of the printed octal pair.  Writing ".0" below is a
! CONVENTION and no !CHECK on this page can see the bits under it
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE",
! item 1).  The ".40" values ARE read, because bit 18 is checked.
!
! PEX1    216712.40  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p136.strap line 37.
!                    Line 2 prints "216712.50 00": 0o50 has the top
!                    bit set, so the .40 half word is observed.
PEX1    SYN, (8)216712.40
! PCOM    216727.40  LANDED, p.136 line 55.  Line 5 prints
!                    "216727.50 00", same reading.
PCOM    SYN, (8)216727.40
! PMAIN   216706.40  LANDED, p.136 line 32.  Line 16's branch half
!                    prints "216706.74 00"; 0o74 has bit 18 set.
PMAIN   SYN, (8)216706.40
! PMAIN1  216707.00  LANDED, p.136 line 33.  Line 18 prints
!                    "216707.10 00"; 0o10's top bit is clear.
PMAIN1  SYN, (8)216707.0
! PERREX  216640.40  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p135.strap line 32,
!                    which transcribes the DEFINING card
!                    "216640.40  215470.40 80  P ERR EX  SIC,
!                    S COM IC".  Lines 8 and 11 print "216640.50 00"
!                    -- the same .40 half word, twice.
PERREX  SYN, (8)216640.40
! SICBU   215361.00  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p116.strap line 24,
!                    which transcribes the defining card "215361.00
!                    000000.00+  S IC BU  VF, 0".  Lines 3 and 17
!                    print "215361.00 30" -- and note the page
!                    spells it BOTH ways, "S IC BU" on line 3 and
!                    "SICBU" on line 17, which is STRAP ignoring
!                    blanks in a name.
SICBU   SYN, (8)215361.0
! GKONEC  217063.00  LANDED as a SYN by p.136 (its line 1's SIC).
!                    Lines 49 and 54 print "217063.36 C2" and
!                    "217063.32 40": 0o36 and 0o32 both have bit 18
!                    clear, so .00.  The volume OCR carries the
!                    DEFINING card on printed p.138, "217063.00
!                    217063.10 00  G KONEC  B,$".
GKONEC  SYN, (8)217063.0
! GVRAT   217063.40  NEW.  Line 44 prints "217063.76 C2"; 0o76 has
!                    bit 18 SET, so the target is the .40 half word
!                    and that bit IS observable and IS checked.  The
!                    volume OCR carries the defining card one line
!                    below G KONEC's on printed p.138: "217063.40
!                    217012.13 04  G VRAT  KVI,$5, G MISTO
!                    - IS BACKSPACE CHAR THE 1ST CHAR."  G KONEC at
!                    .00 and G VRAT at .40 are the two halves of one
!                    word, which is why the two addresses agree to
!                    the word and differ only in bit 18.
GVRAT   SYN, (8)217063.40
! PCDEF2  10.0  >>> AN INTEGER, NOT AN ADDRESS, AND ITS DEFINING
!               CARD WAS FOUND BY GREPPING THE OCR. <<<  Line 12
!               prints "000012.03 01" -- 0o03 is register 1 times
!               two plus one, so the whole printed octal pair is
!               consumed by the register and the half-word select,
!               and the VALUE is the word field, 0o12 = 10.  printed
!               p.100 (PDF p.107) carries the defining card and it
!               was RENDERED, not just OCR'd: at 1800 dpi it reads
!               "-  PCDEF2  SYN,10.0   -DEBUGGER CONSOLE IOD", with
!               the C wide open on the right and the D beside it
!               closed with a straight vertical left stem, so the
!               C/D pair is settled on a DIFFERENT page's ink.  Its
!               neighbours on that page are the same family -- "JC
!               IOD SYN,8.0  -COMMAND CONSOLE IOD", which is the
!               symbol p.136 line 26 uses and which p.136 could only
!               give as 0o10 = 8 from its own print.  So p.100 is
!               the table of console IOD indices and this page uses
!               the debugger's.  Line 13 adds S BAMCP to it, exactly
!               as p.136 line 27 does with JC IOD.
PCDEF2  SYN, 10.0
! SBAMCP  215003.00  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p103.strap !SYM
!                    line 31.  Line 13 prints "215003.02 B0".
SBAMCP  SYN, (8)215003.0
! SCOMRG  215001.00  LANDED, p.103 line 8.  Carried only so that
!                    S L below can be written as p.103 writes it.
SCOMRG  SYN, (8)215001.0
! SL      215001.04  LANDED, p.103 line 14, "S L SYN(BU,1),
!                    S COM RG + 0.04".  FULLY OBSERVED HERE: line 16
!                    is a Branch on Bit, so its printed "215001.04"
!                    is a BIT address and bit 4 is read, not
!                    conventional.  It is the same bit p.136 line 53
!                    tests.
SL      SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG+0.04
! The remaining two are VFL FIELD DEFINITIONS, not addresses, and
! are copied VERBATIM from the landed decks that define them --
! the SYN's mode and length are part of what the referencing rows
! compile.
! SSEL    0.39  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p99.strap line 32
!               "-SELECT FILE STATUS", and carried at this value by
!               p.136.  Line 1 prints "777777.47 8B" for
!               "S SEL-1.0($11)": 0o47 = 39 is the bit, and 777777
!               is -1 word in eighteen bits -- the word BELOW the
!               S SEL that p.136 lines 36 and 37 address.  Fully
!               observed.
SSEL    SYN(BU,1), 0.39
! SFIAAC  3.0   LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p101.strap line 13.
!               Line 15 prints "000003.33 3C" -- SV of register 13,
!               0o33 = 27 = 13*2+1 -- so the WORD 3 is read off the
!               page.
SFIAAC  SYN(BU,18), 3.0

        SLC, (8)216731.0

        CM1111(BU,1),SSEL-1.0($11)      ! line  1  -UNSELECT MCP UNIT
        B,PEX1                          ! line  2
PMPLEV  LV,$0,SICBU                     ! line  3  -INTERRUPT AT MCP LEVEL CONT OF
!                                       ! line  4  -S IC BU   (comment card)
        B,PCOM                          ! line  5
GERROR  LVI,$1,PUNNOT                   ! line  6
        LVI,$2,6.0                      ! line  7
        B,PERREX                        ! line  8
GODBOC  LVI,$1,PMSGER                   ! line  9
        LVI,$2,3.0                      ! line 10
        B,PERREX                        ! line 11
PDBRET  LVI,$1,PCDEF2                   ! line 12
        V+,$1,SBAMCP                    ! line 13
        LV,$13,.32($1)                  ! line 14
        SV,13,SFIAAC($12)               ! line 15  -ACTIVATED FILE AREA ADDR TO UA
        BZB,SL,PMAIN                    ! line 16
        LV,$0,SICBU                     ! line 17  -ENTER AT MCP LEVEL CONTENTS OF SICBU
        B,PMAIN1                        ! line 18
        CNOP                            ! line 19
PTEMBF  DR(BU,64),(13)                  ! line 20
PPPBF   DR(BU,64),(13)                  ! line 21
PMCPBF  DR(BU,64),(13)                  ! line 22
GMISTO  DR(BU,64),(10)                  ! line 23
PEPGK   (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED EPGK ERROR*
PUK     (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED UK ERROR  *
PUNNOT  (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE NOT ACCEPTED PP HAS NO CONSOLE IOD     *
        CNOP                            ! line 27  (emits nothing; already aligned)
PMSGER  (IQS*)DD(BU), MESSAGE HAS BEEN ERASED*
!                                       ! lines 29-37  nine comment cards, G EDIT's contract
GEDIT   Z,$4                            ! line 38  -INITIALIZE
        LVI,$4,PTEMBF+3.0               ! line 39  -FWA OF UNEDITED MSG. IN $4
        LVI,$5,GMISTO                   ! line 40  -FWA FOR EDITED MSG. IN $5
        LCI,$5,80                       ! line 41  -SET MAX ALLOWABLE CHARS TO 80
GOKOLO  L(V+I)(BU,8),.8($4)             ! line 42  -LOAD A CHAR.
        KFI(BU,8),(2)1111 1100          ! line 43  -IS IT BACKSPACE
        BAE,GVRAT                       ! line 44
        ST(V+I)(BU,8),.8($5)            ! line 45  -NO-STORE CHAR IN EDITED BUFFER
        C+I,$4,1                        ! line 46  -ADD ONE TO COUNT OF EDITED
!                                       ! line 47  -CHARS.   (comment card)
        KFI(BU,8),(2)1111 1110          ! line 48  -END CODE
        BAE,GKONEC                      ! line 49  -YES-OUT
        CB,$5,GOKOLO                    ! line 50
GKONTR  KCI,$4,80                       ! line 51  -HAVE EDITED 80 CHARS WITHOUT
!                                       ! lines 52-53  -ENCOUNTERING- / -AND END CODE
        BZXL,GKONEC                     ! line 54  -IS EDITED CHARACTER COUNT LESS
!                                       ! line 55  -THAN 80   (comment card)
        SC,$4,$R                        ! line 56  -YES-PUT EDITED CHAR.COUNT IN SR

! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PRINTED LISTING, read off PDF p.144 (400 dpi for the page and
! the card serials, 600 dpi for the message cards' blank runs, 900
! dpi for the BINARY OUTPUT column, 1200 dpi for the NAME column,
! 1800 dpi for the glyphs of G ODBOC, G OKOLO and P DBRET -- and for
! P CDEF2 on printed p.100, a different page).
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!  LINE  LOCATION       BINARY OUTPUT                   NAME      STATEMENT                                            COMMENT                                   CARD
!   1*   216731.00      777777.47 8B  001000.36 F0                CM1111(BU,1),S SEL-1.0($11)                          -UNSELECT MCP UNIT                        EE000111
!   2*   216732.00      216712.50 00                              B,PEX1                                                                                         EE000112
!   3*   216732.40      215361.00 30                    P MP LEV  LV,$0,S IC BU                                        -INTERRUPT AT MCP LEVEL CONT OF           EE000113
!   4*                                             -                                                                   -S IC BU                                  EE000114
!   5*   216733.00      216727.50 00                              B,P COM                                                                                        EE000115
!   6*   216733.40      217034.03 01                    G ERROR   LVI,$1, P UN NOT                                                                               EE000116
!   7*   216734.00      000006.05 01                              LVI,$2, 6.0                                                                                    EE000117
!   8*   216734.40      216640.50 00                              B, P ERR EX                                                                                    EE000118
!   9*   216735.00      217042.03 01                    G ODBOC   LVI,$1, P MSG ER                                                                               EE000119
!  10*   216735.40      000003.05 01                              LVI,$2, 3.0                                                                                    EE000120
!  11*   216736.00      216640.50 00                              B, P ERR EX                                                                                    EE000121
!  12*   216736.40      000012.03 01                    PDBRET    LVI,$1,PCDEF2                                                                                  EE000122
!  13*   216737.00      215003.02 B0                              V+,$1,SBAMCP                                                                                   EE000123
!  14*   216737.40      000000.72 31                              LV,$13,.32($1)                                                                                 EE000124
